<article class="disney"><header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Walt Disney World</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">Disney</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">−44% exit rate</span><span class="case-meta__role">Senior UX Strategist</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Research-Led UX &amp; Information Architecture</p><h1>Public Affairs<br /><span class="heading-accent">Digital</span><br />Transformation</h1></div><p>Walt Disney World’s Public Affairs website served journalists, community organizations, and people interested in its local impact. Through Voce Communications and Porter Novelli, I led the research, information architecture, wireframes, and visual design to help those audiences find the right information and complete their tasks.</p></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "Discovery"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Information architecture"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Delivery"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Results"}]} /><figure class="hero"><img src="/images/disney_hero.webp" alt="Cinderella Castle at Walt Disney World" loading="eager" decoding="async" /></figure>
<section class="disney-detail" aria-labelledby="disney-brief"><div><p class="disney-detail__label">The brief</p><h2 id="disney-brief">Prioritize the visitors who needed support.</h2></div><div class="disney-detail__copy"><p>The project’s initial analysis identified donation seekers as 43% of engaged traffic, yet their request path was buried in the navigation. A reported 77% category-page bounce rate added to the evidence that visitors were struggling to reach relevant content.</p><p>Journalists faced a separate problem: releases, photos, and videos appeared together without a clear way to browse by type. The redesign needed to make those different tasks visible and give each audience a practical route through the site.</p></div></section>
<section class="research" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p>01 / Discovery</p><h2>Three journeys.<br />One hidden service.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>I audited 12 Fortune 500 Public Affairs websites and three Orlando theme park properties. The comparison focused on how each site organized press materials, community support, and impact stories. I also reviewed 90 days of heatmap data to identify where the existing navigation was being missed.</p><p>I ran moderated usability sessions with eight participants across the three audience groups. Some could not distinguish the Public Affairs site from the main resort experience. Others missed the navigation or did not recognize that they had moved to a different section. These findings pointed to problems with hierarchy and orientation.</p></div><div class="research__images"><figure><img src="/images/disney01.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs desktop design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney02.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs mobile design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney03-1.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs wireframe exploration" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="architecture" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><div class="architecture__head"><p>02 / Information architecture</p><h2>Make the task<br /><span class="heading-accent">the entry point.</span></h2></div><div class="copy"><p>I separated the content into three journeys: press materials for media professionals, support information for community organizations, and impact stories for general stakeholders. Static information and frequently published press content received distinct structures rather than competing within one stream.</p><p>I also made the terminology consistent across sections. Labels such as Stories, News, and Releases had overlapped without clearly explaining the difference. The revised taxonomy and content-type categories gave visitors a more predictable way to browse, while modular homepage sections accommodated changing priorities.</p></div><figure><img src="/images/disney04.webp" alt="Disney information architecture wireframes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></section>
<section class="implementation" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p>03 / Responsive delivery</p><h2>A Shorter Flow.<br />A Clearer Interface.</h2></div><div class="implementation__images"><figure><img src="/images/disney05-1.webp" alt="Disney mobile navigation design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney07.webp" alt="Disney responsive UI system" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div><div class="copy"><p>I reduced the donation request path from more than five interactions to two. In follow-up testing, all five returning participants completed the task on their first attempt. The original round had recorded two successful completions among eight participants. The smaller follow-up group offered encouraging evidence for the revised flow, rather than a population-wide success rate.</p><p>The visual system drew on Disney Parks’ internal brand guidelines while giving Public Affairs a clear identity. On mobile, donation requests and featured community stories took priority. I supplied design specifications and reviewed builds against the intended layouts and interactions; the development team handled front-end implementation.</p></div></section>
<section class="disney-detail" aria-labelledby="disney-ownership"><div><p class="disney-detail__label">Scope &amp; collaboration</p><h2 id="disney-ownership">Carry the research decisions into delivery.</h2></div><div class="disney-detail__copy"><p>I owned the research plan, usability sessions, information architecture, wireframes, and visual design system. The priority was to connect the decisions made in research to the actual navigation and page hierarchy, especially for the donation journey.</p><p>The development team implemented the front end. My role during delivery was to provide specifications and review the builds against the design intent, keeping the task paths and visual hierarchy consistent as the designs became working pages.</p></div></section>
<section class="results" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><div><p>04 / Results</p><h2>Results &amp;<br />Impact Metrics</h2></div><div class="numbers"><div><b>44%</b><span>decrease in site-wide exit rate</span></div><div><b>24%</b><span>decrease in category-page bounce rate</span></div><div><b>26%</b><span>increase in session duration</span></div></div><div class="copy"><p>The original project account reports a 44% decrease in site-wide exit rate, a 24% decrease in category-page bounce rate, and a 26% increase in session duration after launch. These are reported site-level outcomes. They do not isolate the contribution of an individual navigation or visual design change.</p><p>The shorter donation path addressed a specific problem observed during research. The follow-up task sessions helped assess that change, while the wider analytics described how the site was being used after launch. I would use both sources to guide further research, rather than treating either as a complete picture.</p></div></section><section class="disney-detail" aria-labelledby="disney-reflection"><div><p class="disney-detail__label">Reflection</p><h2 id="disney-reflection">Observe community partners using the mobile site.</h2></div><div class="disney-detail__copy"><p>I would add a dedicated round of field research with community partners. Their mobile usage patterns were inferred from analytics, rather than observed directly in the situations where they needed the site.</p><p>Watching people find support information and begin a request on their own phones would help test the mobile priorities. It could also reveal interruptions, terminology questions, and practical constraints that a moderated session might miss.</p></div></section></article>
